WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
<v Mike>Bill Elliott's NASCAR Challenge, experience life in the fast lane.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:28.660
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.520
<v Hosts>I'm Mike.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.660
<v Hosts>And I'm Sean.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.980
<v Mike>And I'm Bill Elliott.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.280
<v Mike>Now, could you imagine?

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.020
<v Sean>What a guest spot. Is he still around?

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.560
<v Mike>I don't know. I didn't look up before the episode, so maybe that was in bad taste.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.800
<v Sean>That's okay. He goes fast.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:45.660
<v Sean>We all know it.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.880
<v Mike>Yeah. Yeah, he's actually, he is speed.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:50.760
<v Sean>Is that what he said?

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.820
<v Mike>No, that's, what's his name from Cars?

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:55.800
<v Mike>Lightning McQueen?

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.420
<v Mike>He's like, I am speed.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.160
<v Mike>It's like, I feel like every NASCAR driver should say that if they grew up watching Cars.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.200
<v Mike>Because those kids that grew up watching Cars are now driving the Cars.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.720
<v Sean>And if he ain't first, you're last.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.420
<v Mike>Yeah, that was probably in Cars 2.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.980
<v Sean>Well, that's Talladega Nights.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:14.680
<v Sean>Oh, perfect.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.960
<v Mike>Yes, of course. I was thinking it was Dodgeball for some reason.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.280
<v Sean>Yeah, same thing. Similar vibe.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:27.660
<v Mike>Yeah. Now, we've kind of had a similar vibe to this kind of game before when we played Days of Thunder, the game based on the movie.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:28.500
<v Mike>Yes.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:36.460
<v Mike>And that movie had the NASCAR, had the NASCAR license and obviously all took place in the NASCAR environment.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:45.580
<v Mike>But the game did not. And so this is now the first time that a racing game, of which we've had many, they don't usually go for the realistic.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:54.560
<v Mike>But here we are with Bill Elliott's NASCAR Challenge, NASCAR in the title on the starting screen.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:03.100
<v Mike>Right. On the starting screen, the NASCAR logo beautifully recreated here in 8-bit.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:12.920
<v Mike>So this is like a big deal because at this point, I feel like every other major sport has been represented, right?

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.560
<v Sean>Maybe not the NBA, actually.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.960
<v Mike>Did we get an NBA game?

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.360
<v Sean>Well, we got some NBA players.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.300
<v Sean>I don't know. I think that counts, right?

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.240
<v Mike>Yeah. Michael Jordan, if Jordan and Bird are version each other, that's basically the whole NBA at that point anyway.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.260
<v Sean>Yeah, exactly. But no, I don't think we actually got an NBA game yet.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.060
<v Sean>Anyway, NASCAR, do you have any history with NASCAR?

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.040
<v Mike>I don't have any history with NASCAR.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.800
<v Mike>I have watched a few races.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.420
<v Mike>It is a thing you can do, especially with a drink.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.660
<v Sean>Yeah, nobody tells you to not do it.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:50.680
<v Sean>You can just do it.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.600
<v Mike>Yeah, yeah. So I have watched a few.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:59.900
<v Mike>Don't think I understand enough about like, or maybe I do understand enough and that's why I'm out of it.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.800
<v Mike>But to me, both F1 and NASCAR have this problem, which is unavoidable.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:05.940
<v Mike>There's really no way around it.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.060
<v Mike>They can't drive while the cars are like fucked up on the track.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:16.700
<v Mike>But when that happens, it causes a, you know, a safety car to come out and everybody has to slow down.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:22.640
<v Mike>And, you know, any like sort of lead that was gained is now like, you know, you still keep your position.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.940
<v Mike>Yeah. But you now have to drive slowly around.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.960
<v Mike>And I've seen NASCAR races where they just end on the safety lap.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.900
<v Mike>And that just feels like such bullshit.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:43.220
<v Mike>Yeah. I just, it's like, that would be like if, you know, in the, well, I guess actually the equivalent, the closest I can think of is in the NFL when they just can have the ability to just run the clock.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.520
<v Sean>Like, no, I'm not playing anymore.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:45.820
<v Sean>You can just kneel. You're just going to kneel.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.840
<v Sean>Yeah. It's funny.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:56.500
<v Sean>I grew up with NASCAR on in the house, but I don't think anyone really gave a shit.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:05.960
<v Sean>Like even like my, my stepdad who would have it on didn't seem to be reacting in any way to whatever was happening in the race.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.520
<v Sean>It was just, he was just on his rocking chair, just staring at the screen.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.080
<v Sean>And I was just like, oh, NASCAR.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:15.220
<v Sean>And he'd be like, yep.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.700
<v Sean>And he would just kind of look at it.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.780
<v Sean>And that's just sort of the relationship that I developed with it.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.900
<v Sean>Like I never really, unless something was actually crashing into another thing.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.480
<v Sean>Um, I wasn't paying too much attention to NASCAR.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:37.740
<v Mike>Now to defend the NASCAR fans out there, I don't want them to think that we think that, uh, we're objectively correct here.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.440
<v Mike>We just didn't grow up in the NASCAR landscape.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.840
<v Mike>Like there's no NASCAR races coming to Buffalo.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.260
<v Sean>Uh, and there were certainly none coming to Long Island.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.580
<v Sean>Yeah, but they come to Lockheed Glen. And that's right between both of us.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.920
<v Mike>Okay. But you, but would you go?

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.280
<v Sean>I would go.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.340
<v Sean>I have been to a Talladega race.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.220
<v Mike>I, okay.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:55.160
<v Mike>So this is exciting.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:05:10.500
<v Mike>Cause that's what I was going to ask you next is like, I was going to say that to me, the, the core appeal of a NASCAR race cannot be captured on TV for something that we were talking about in the supercars episode, which is that like, you know, you just can't capture that sense of speed.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:24.100
<v Mike>It's like, it's kind of unbelievable how fast those cars are going, but when they're all going that speed and the cameras tracking them all on TV, it's just not nearly as exciting as just hearing and feeling and being there.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.740
<v Mike>You know? Yeah, exactly.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.200
<v Mike>That is, that's like the difference.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:29.760
<v Mike>That's the NASCAR experience.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:35.320
<v Sean>Yeah. So when I went, I was way too young to really fully take advantage of it.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:47.180
<v Sean>Like if I went there in my twenties, it probably would have been like a near religious experience, um, with just how centered, uh, the drinking culture is there.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.540
<v Sean>But, um, yeah, it was very fast.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.940
<v Sean>Uh, they, they really get back past you.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:06:05.360
<v Sean>It was very loud. Um, but I'd say after the first 15 or so laps, it does kind of get like hypnotic and like in a bad way, kind of repetitive.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.460
<v Sean>And you're sort of waiting, like how long does this go?

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:14.440
<v Sean>Um, but I was too young to fully enjoy the atmosphere.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:29.080
<v Mike>And while this is believed to be the very first video game to ever use the NASCAR license, I thought what was more interesting is that as the, um, after the opening screen with the NASCAR splash, you have, uh, the credits for the game.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:30.840
<v Mike>It has the logo for distinctive software.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:35.860
<v Mike>And in the credits, in the design and program section is Bill Elliott.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.800
<v Mike>Like that's the kind of thing that like you usually put as like a special thanks or a featuring.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.580
<v Mike>Right. But he's like, I'm supposed to believe he coded the game.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:49.340
<v Sean>I have to imagine that like, I don't know.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.760
<v Sean>There, there's something about now to actually talk about the game.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.140
<v Sean>There is something that feels very authentic about this game.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:10.700
<v Sean>I don't know if it's because it actually feels like a bit more realistic than most, whether it's visually or, um, the way that the vehicles handle, but like they, anytime you have to tune a car.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:11.880
<v Sean>Yeah. In a video game.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:16.600
<v Mike>I'm automatically sold that it's more realistic than the ones that just let you hop in and do automatic.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:23.300
<v Sean>Well, I completely like ignored all of the shit I could sort of, uh, mess around with.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:24.640
<v Sean>Cause I didn't know what any of it meant.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:30.320
<v Sean>Um, I, I, but I made sure to use manual because I'm not a coward.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.540
<v Mike>Um, but like, can you, can you say a little more about that, Sean, though?

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:42.180
<v Mike>Cause you gave an interesting perspective to me when we were, um, at long Island retro gaming expo, uh, when we were doing the arcade racers.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:47.560
<v Mike>And obviously those have, you know, they have a wheel, they have pedals, they have, um, the gearbox.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:55.360
<v Mike>So you, you said something very interesting about your philosophy behind choosing manual, even though you would not drive a manual car in real life.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.720
<v Sean>I don't entirely remember what I told you.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:05.980
<v Mike>Um, I know, I know we talked about this, but you said, what's the point of, of playing a racing video game?

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:10.800
<v Mike>If, if I'm not like, it's like, it's basically like having at that point, why steer too, right?

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:22.920
<v Mike>Like if it's going to do the gears for you and what, what are, you know, I totally agree because for the longest time I was an automatic transition, uh, kid growing up just because I couldn't even understand the concept because we didn't own a manual car.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:31.000
<v Mike>But once I like got into it, I was like, not only does it make you a faster, better driver in these games, but it's also just like, it's more immersive.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:35.780
<v Mike>Yeah. You kind of, you kind of have to, like, even if you do a bad job, it's like you're a bad driver then.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:48.840
<v Sean>Exactly. And I'm not going to blow a trans, well, you could blow your transmission in this game, but I'm not blowing my transit transmission, uh, and then having to spend thousands of dollars to replace it.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.220
<v Sean>I just have to do the race again.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:55.400
<v Sean>Um, so yeah, it's, it's fun.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:11.640
<v Sean>So, I mean, that's all to say that I totally buy that Bill Elliott or one of Bill Elliott's people advised on maybe some like game feel or like systems in this.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:19.760
<v Sean>Cause like, I just don't, it feels way too good compared to a lot of other racers that we've played.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.700
<v Mike>Yeah. I would definitely agree there, especially on the simulation front.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:31.280
<v Mike>I think the closest thing we have is the F1 counterpart, um, Al Unser Jr.'s, uh, turbo racing, I think was the name of the game.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:33.060
<v Sean>Was that F1 or was that Indy?

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.260
<v Mike>Oh, right. Maybe that was Indy.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:39.120
<v Mike>Yeah. Sorry. Um, now I can't, or was that the one that you work up to F1?

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.040
<v Mike>Like is the last car you can get an F1 car?

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.080
<v Sean>Oh, that's a good question.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:44.240
<v Mike>I do remember that one too.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:45.500
<v Sean>I think that's different.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:47.520
<v Mike>Maybe I'm merging two games together.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:53.600
<v Mike>Yeah. Anyway, that was, you know, this has never been done with these kinds of cars and this kind of speed behind them and everything.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.180
<v Mike>So I do agree with you.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:59.860
<v Mike>Um, and I will just say too, it's like, it's the kind of thing that is intimidating at the same time.

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:09.980
<v Mike>Like part of the appeal of having Rad Racer on the NES is that you can just literally be four years old, plug in that game and get the concept of trying to win a race.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:18.960
<v Mike>Like that, that, that, that comes across easy now with the tuning and you know, the difference between manual and your engine and all that and the different cars.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:32.040
<v Mike>And then like learning the track layouts, doing the qualifying laps, like this is definitely, um, a step above in the polish of a racing game, but it is also an intimidating kind of racing game as a result.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:44.560
<v Mike>Do you think that this is the kind of thing that, um, you know, that, uh, you can just jump into and play or do you kind of, do you kind of suck at first for a long time before you get good?

00:10:44.560 --> 00:11:02.260
<v Sean>Well, well, Mike, the first thing that happened when I got into the race was I immediately just tailspinned and I had everybody blow by me because I didn't entirely know how to accelerate slash shift.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:08.460
<v Sean>So the first turn that you take is just going to make you lose complete control.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:10.460
<v Sean>That was what, that was my experience.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:14.160
<v Sean>Um, and then, yeah, it took a few more tries.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.800
<v Sean>To even like clear a turn without hitting the wall.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.440
<v Sean>Uh, and then I, then I decided I'm playing on hard mode.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:28.460
<v Sean>I got to get out of Watkins Glen road courses probably shouldn't be the first thing you do.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:33.320
<v Sean>So then I moved to just a typical like oval ish circuit.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:36.340
<v Sean>Like I think I did Daytona first.

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:41.020
<v Sean>Um, and there was a little bit easier, but I'm still hitting the wall.

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:43.440
<v Sean>I'm still, I'm still wiping out.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:51.840
<v Sean>Um, but I'd say it took like six or seven races to even get a good, a good feel for the game.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:59.560
<v Mike>Yeah. I spent a lot of time in the qualifying, um, just to kind of even learn, you know, you know, cause it's NASCAR too.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.400
<v Mike>It's like, am I, am I breaking?

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:04.140
<v Mike>Like, am I just always supposed to be accelerating?

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:05.740
<v Mike>Like, how do I do the turns?

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:09.820
<v Mike>You know, I know that the, the turns are also very forgiving in these kinds of tracks.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.280
<v Mike>It's not like indie racing.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:19.840
<v Mike>So I just was like, I needed to have that kind of, um, cause it's a, it's a first person view through the driver's seat.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:35.460
<v Mike>So it's maybe a little easier in like a less realistic game, like, um, Daytona USA, where you have this third person POV and you can see the track, your car, how you're turning and everything in, in this cockpit, which is again, super immersive.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:43.000
<v Mike>And you're able to see even like your little rear view, uh, mirror up at the top and you have your, your temperature and how much gas you have left.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.100
<v Mike>Like all, all these things are amazing and they all function too.

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:54.620
<v Mike>Yeah. But it's the kind of thing where it's like, I just, I don't know if I know enough yet to like do a race and, and place properly.

00:12:54.620 --> 00:13:00.660
<v Mike>And I think maybe that's where like the simulator got, like got too into it.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:06.520
<v Mike>Like it needed to have more of like a, we'll get to the really challenging stuff that only skilled people can do.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:11.020
<v Mike>But we also need to make sure that like, if you buy this game, it's not just wasted on you.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.380
<v Sean>If you don't understand NASCAR racing.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:29.500
<v Sean>Yeah. I think the first time I was completely caught off guard with what it expected me to do was when I, like first I wasn't expecting to get the low fuel, uh, notification that quickly or the alarm for that.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:37.780
<v Sean>And then I was like, Hmm, I don't remember seeing a pit stop, but I'll just look for something weird on the side of the road.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:43.180
<v Sean>And then on this next lap, um, then I found it and then I'm like, okay, there's going to be some cut scene.

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:48.180
<v Sean>That's my team working on my car and I can just take a breather.

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:54.220
<v Sean>And then it brings you to a completely other like game mode that is top down.

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:56.000
<v Sean>So you're no longer first person.

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:58.540
<v Sean>It's like you're playing spy hunter now.

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:08.580
<v Sean>Um, and you have to like find your pit or your pit crew and stop in like right in a parking spot.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:10.480
<v Sean>And you can just blow right by them.

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:15.740
<v Sean>Like if you weren't paying attention or know that this was coming, you would have no idea what to do.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:16.940
<v Sean>I guess we didn't read the manual.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:23.600
<v Sean>Um, so, uh, that didn't work out quite the way I wanted it to the first time I tried.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.380
<v Sean>I was able to kind of get it the next time.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:35.500
<v Sean>Uh, but yeah, uh, then you have to pick what kind of work you want done and then you have to decide when enough's enough and start going.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:45.220
<v Sean>Then it gives you a little bit of like, hey, can you make up some positions if you have other people in the, uh, the pit with you?

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.320
<v Sean>It's a lot to do that.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:48.680
<v Sean>I just was not prepared for.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:53.460
<v Mike>Right. And, and, you know, we should specify that there are difficulties to choose from.

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:55.480
<v Mike>Uh, there starts on rookie.

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:56.400
<v Sean>Easiest. Yeah.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:15:02.640
<v Mike>It starts on rookie. You can move even down below to novice and novice makes it so that, uh, you cannot crash.

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:10.880
<v Mike>Oh. Uh, which I, I guess is a useful feature if you want to play bumper cars against the, the rails and just hit the walls every time on the turns.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:12.780
<v Mike>Uh, an acceptable strategy.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:19.380
<v Mike>Maybe you will obviously lose your speed, uh, by doing that, but that's like, that's Gran Turismo, uh, shit.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:23.020
<v Mike>It's kind of funny that they still don't allow those cars to get beat up or anything.

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:25.660
<v Sean>You can't show our car with damage, Mike.

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:37.520
<v Mike>Right. Uh, and then the pro version, um, which is the step up from rookie, uh, is, is basically the same thing except for now the, uh, the AI, it's not necessarily that they're smarter.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:42.800
<v Mike>It's that they drive faster, apparently, according to the, um, to the manual here.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:53.000
<v Mike>So that's an interesting like update is that like, they're both competent drivers at the rookie and pro level, but specifically at the pro level, they just like are no longer like put in their pants.

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.000
<v Mike>Pinky toe on the gas.

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:55.520
<v Mike>Yeah. They're finally going all out.

00:15:56.140 --> 00:15:59.020
<v Mike>Um, that to say, of course, I never tried a pro race.

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:00.960
<v Mike>I, I wasn't up to the, to the challenge.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:11.180
